.TH MAILX 1 "August 2026" "Bashy" "POSIX Commands"
.SH NAME
mailx, mail \- process a local mailbox and send local messages
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mailx
.RB [ -eHiNn ]
.RB [ -F ]
.RB [ -u
.IR user ]
.RB [ -f
.RI [ file ]]
.RB [ -b
.IR address ]
.RB [ -c
.IR address ]
.RB [ -r
.IR address ]
.RB [ -s
.IR subject ]
.RB [ -t ]
.RI [ address ...]
.br
.B mail
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ address ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mailx
reads messages from a local mbox or composes a message for local operating-system
accounts.  The Bashy implementation intentionally provides the POSIX local-mail
interface only: it neither contacts SMTP nor invokes an external mail transfer
agent.  Delivery appends a locked, escaped mbox record to the recipient's local
spool file.
.PP
With one or more address operands, standard input is the message body.  With no
address operands,
.B mailx
enters command mode on the selected mailbox.  Message numbers and the special
current-message selectors are accepted by commands that operate on messages.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B -e
Test for mail.  Exit successfully when the selected mailbox contains at least
one message, without starting command mode.
.TP
.B -f [file]
Read the named mbox instead of the system mailbox.  If
.I file
is omitted, use the user's secondary mbox.
.TP
.B -F
Record sent mail in a file named for the first recipient.
.TP
.BI -b " address"
Send a blind copy to each named local address.  Multiple addresses may be
separated by commas or shell whitespace.
.TP
.BI -c " address"
Send a copy to each named local address.
.TP
.B -H
Write header summaries and exit.
.TP
.B -i
Ignore interrupts while reading a message body.
.TP
.B -N
Do not write the initial header summary in command mode.
.TP
.B -n
Do not read the system startup file.
.TP
.BI -r " address"
Set the return address for a composed local message.
.TP
.BI -s " subject"
Set the Subject header.
.TP
.B -t
Read recipients from the To, Cc, and Bcc fields of the message headers in
standard input.  Any address operands are combined with those header recipients.
.TP
.BI -u " user"
Read the local system mailbox of
.IR user .
.SH COMMAND MODE
The POSIX command language includes message display and navigation; header
listing; deletion and undelete; save, copy, and write; reply and Reply; mail;
preserve; folder selection; variable set and unset; shell escape; source;
version; help; and quit or exit.  Commands accept message lists where specified.
On normal quit, deleted messages are removed and retained messages are written
back atomically; exit abandons mailbox changes.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/mail/user
Default local system mailbox.  The root may be changed with
.BR MAILX_SPOOL .
.TP
.I $HOME/mbox
Default secondary mailbox.
.TP
.I $HOME/.mailrc
Per-user startup commands.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.BR DEAD ,
.BR EDITOR ,
.BR HOME ,
.BR LISTER ,
.BR MAIL ,
.BR MAILRC ,
.BR MBOX ,
.BR PAGER ,
.BR SHELL ,
.BR TMPDIR ,
and
.B VISUAL
select POSIX mailx files and helpers.  Locale variables affect character
handling and diagnostics.  Bashy's
.B MAILX_SPOOL
extension selects the root of the local-only spool.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero indicates successful delivery or mailbox processing.  Greater than zero
indicates invalid syntax, an unavailable local account or mailbox, a lock or
I/O failure, rejected message data, or another error.
.SH SECURITY
Mailbox updates use advisory locking and atomic replacement where required.
Recipient names are local account names, not network addresses.  Header input
is validated to prevent injection and mbox body lines are escaped on delivery.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ed (1),
.BR sh (1)
.SH STANDARDS
POSIX.1-2008, 2016 Edition (Issue 7).  Remote transport is outside the required
POSIX interface and is deliberately absent.
